{
  "title": "Working of the heart",
  "topic": "Working of the heart",
  "turns": [
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0001",
      "speaker": "student",
      "text": "Teach me about the human heart\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0002",
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "text": "❤️ The human heart – let's learn it simply!\nThe human heart is one of the most important organs in our body. It acts like a natural blood pump.\nLet's learn about the heart step by step, starting with the basics.\nLesson 1: Where is the heart located?\nHeart: Anatomy | Concise Medical Knowledge\n\n\n\n\nCaptured diagram\n\n\n\n\n\n\nlecturio.com\n\n\nThe heart is located near the center of our chest, slightly to the left.\nIt is approximately the size of our fist.\n\n\n\nAn adult heart usually weighs 250–350 grams.\n\n\n\nAt rest, an adult heart generally beats 60–100 times per minute.\n\n\n\n\nLesson 2: The four chambers of the heart\nHeart Chambers Diagram — Free Printable Clipart for Teachers | Kuraplan\n\n\n\n\nCaptured diagram\n\n\n\n\n\n\nkuraplan.com\n\n\nThe human heart has four chambers.\nChamber\nFunction\n\nRight Atrium\nReceives oxygen-poor blood returning from the body\n\nRight Ventricle\nSends blood to the lungs\n\nLeft Atrium\nReceives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs\n\nLeft Ventricle\nSends blood throughout the body\n\n\n\nImportant point: The muscular wall of the left ventricle is thick because it must pump blood under pressure throughout the body.\nLesson 3: How does blood circulate?\nBody organs\nOxygen-poor blood\nCaptured diagram\n\nThrough the veins\n\nRight side of the heart\nRight atrium → right ventricle\n\nCaptured diagram\n\nPulmonary artery\n\nCaptured diagram\n\nLungs\nBlood receives oxygen\nCaptured diagram\n\nPulmonary veins\n\nLeft side of the heart\nLeft atrium → left ventricle\n\nCaptured diagram\n\nAorta\n\nBack throughout the body\nOxygen-rich blood\n\nThis cycle continues throughout our lives.\nLesson 4: Why does the heart beat?\nThe contraction and relaxation of the heart muscles produce the heartbeat.\nSystole (contraction): The heart contracts and pumps blood out.\n\n\n\nDiastole (relaxation): The heart relaxes and fills with blood again.\n\n\n\n\nThe heart's natural electrical conduction system coordinates these beats. Its main starting point is called the SA node.\nLesson 5: How can we keep the heart healthy?\nDaily walking, a diet rich in vegetables and fruit, enough sleep, avoiding tobacco, and controlling blood pressure help keep the heart healthy.\n🧠 Short quiz\nLet's check what we have learned!\nQuestion 1 / 3\n0 answered\n\n\n\n\n\nHow many chambers does the human heart have?\n2 chambers\n4 chambers\n6 chambers\n\nPreviousNext question Captured diagram\n\n\n\n\nNext, we can study the heart valves, the electrical system that controls the heartbeat, and how a heart attack occurs in greater detail with pictures.\nWould you prefer to learn about the heart at a school student level or a medical student level?\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0003",
      "speaker": "student",
      "text": "Do all the valves open at the same time?\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0004",
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "text": "No. ❤️ The heart's four valves do not all open at the same time. During different stages of the heartbeat, when two valves are open, the other two are closed.\nThere are four valves in the heart:\nTricuspid valve – between the right atrium and right ventricle.\n\n\n\nMitral valve – between the left atrium and left ventricle.\n\n\n\nPulmonary valve – between the right ventricle and pulmonary artery.\n\n\n\nAortic valve – between the left ventricle and aorta.\n\n\n\n\nCaptured image\n\n\n\n\nCaptured diagram\n\n\n\n\n\n\nmdpi.com\n\n\nWhen do the valves open?\nPhase of the heart\nOpen valves\nClosed valves\n\nDiastole – when the ventricles relax\nTricuspid, Mitral\nPulmonary, Aortic\n\nSystole – when the ventricles pump blood out\nPulmonary, Aortic\nTricuspid, Mitral\n\n\n\nAn important point: There are also brief phases of the heartbeat when all four valves are closed. But all four never open at the same time.\nThis is because blood must flow in only one direction. The valves prevent blood from flowing backward.\nA question for you 🧠\nWhen the heart's ventricles contract (ventricular systole), what does the mitral valve do?\nA. Opens\nB. Closes\nC. Opens and closes\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0005",
      "speaker": "student",
      "text": "Does it purify the blood?\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    },
    {
      "turn_id": "turn_0006",
      "speaker": "tutor",
      "text": "Yes, blood is purified in the body. But ❤️ the heart does not purify blood directly. The heart's main job is to pump blood throughout the body.\nThree main organs are involved in cleaning the blood and maintaining its quality.\nHealthy human lungs. Vector illustration isolated\n\n\n\n\nCaptured diagram\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n1. Lungs\nThey remove carbon dioxide from the blood and add oxygen to it.\n\n\n\nKodėl nuolatinis nuovargis gali būti ženklas, kad jūsų inkstams reikia pagalbos?\n\n\n\n\nCaptured diagram\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2. Kidneys\nThey filter waste products such as urea, along with excess water and salts, from the blood and excrete them as urine.\n\n\n\nInternal liver\n\n\n\n\nCaptured diagram\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3. Liver\nIt processes medicines and some toxins and helps remove them from the body.\n\n\n\n❤️ What is the connection between the heart and lungs?\nOxygen-poor blood from the body reaches the right side of the heart.\n\n\n\nThe heart sends that blood to the lungs.\n\n\n\nIn the lungs, carbon dioxide is removed and oxygen is added.\n\n\n\nOxygen-rich blood returns to the left side of the heart.\n\n\n\nThe heart sends it throughout the body again.\n\n\n\n\nKey points to remember:\n❤️ Heart – pumps blood.\n\n\n\n🫁 Lungs – exchange gases.\n\n\n\nKidneys – filter waste from the blood.\n\n\n\n\nA question for you: Is the blood going to the lungs oxygen-poor or oxygen-rich?\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    }
  ],
